     Every value here was read off the running stock ROM, from
     work/device_dump/display/dumpsys-display.txt, rather than copied from
     another device. The relevant line is the DisplayDeviceInfo for
     "Built-in Screen".

     Nothing panel specific belongs in this file. The panel is dual sourced
     (BOE nt36536e / CSOT nt36536) and is selected by the bootloader on the
     kernel command line, resolved inside the prebuilt dtbo. Both suppliers run
     the same mode list, so these values hold for either one.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- The panel runs 30/60/90/120/144.

         ⚠️ config_defaultRefreshRate was 60 here, on the reasoning that 60 is
         "the rate the display settles to when nothing is driving it". That
         reasoning was inferred from an idle renderFrameRate reading rather than
         read off the resource, and it is wrong. DisplayModeDirector on the
         running stock ROM reports

             mDefaultRefreshRate: 144.0
             mDefaultPeakRefreshRate: 240.0

         mDefaultRefreshRate is R.integer.config_defaultRefreshRate, so stock
         sets it to 144 (via FrameworksResTarget_Vendor.apk, the vendor RRO this
         port replaces). At 60 it posts a PRIORITY_DEFAULT_RENDER_FRAME_RATE
         vote (DisplayModeDirector.java:1225-1227) that caps the render frame
         rate globally at 60 — on a 144 Hz panel.

         ★ Both are 120, not 144, and that is deliberate: THE ACTIVE STYLUS DOES
         NOT WORK AT 144 Hz. This is measured on this unit, both directions, with
         the pen in the owner's hand and /proc/support_pen already set to 1:

             peak_refresh_rate = 120.0   ->  6746 pen events, 438 ABS_PRESSURE
                                             samples over 23 strokes, pressure a
                                             smooth ramp 0x365 -> 0x8a2 -> back
                                             down, and /proc/Pen_ID resolving to
                                             "2;87;..." (pen type 2, battery 87%)
             peak_refresh_rate = 144.0   ->  0 pen events. Not "no pressure" -
                                             zero. /proc/Pen_ID falls straight
                                             back to 255;255;0xffff;0xffff.

         So at 144 Hz the controller cannot even detect the pen, let alone decode
         its uplink. That also rules out any auto-switching scheme that watches
         the digitizer for a pen: at 144 Hz the digitizer is blind, so there is
         nothing to watch. Chicken and egg.

         Stock hits the same wall and solves it in the framework. ZUI patches
         DisplayModeDirector with judgePenSupport()/supportPen(boolean):

             supportPen(true):   updateGlobalVote(26, forRenderFrameRates(0, 120f))
                                 setHighReportRate(0)
             supportPen(false):  updateGlobalVote(26, null)
                                 setHighReportRate(1)

         i.e. it votes the render rate down to exactly 120 whenever the pen is in
         use, and swaps the touch controller between two mutually exclusive
         modes - high finger report rate, or active-pen scanning, never both.
         judgePenSupport() picks between them from Settings.System
         auto_refresh_support_pen_switch (a user toggle) and is_pen_attached,
         the latter fed by BluetoothPenConnectPolicy over the pen's BLE link.

         We have neither that framework patch nor the BLE plumbing, so the choice
         is between "144 Hz and no stylus" and "120 Hz and a working stylus" -
         and this device ships with a stylus. 120 vs 144 is a 1.4 ms difference
         in frame time; a stylus that produces no events at all is not a
         trade-off. Both states this leaves reachable work with the pen: Smooth
         Display on gives 120 (config_defaultPeakRefreshRate), off gives 60.

         Consequence worth stating plainly, because it answers "is our touch
         sampling rate the same as stock": /proc/HighReportRate stays at the
         driver default 0, and that is now CORRECT rather than an omission - it
         is exactly the value stock uses in its supportPen(true) state, which is
         the state this configuration is permanently in. Before this change the
         device was in neither of stock's two states: HighReportRate 0 (stock's
         pen mode) at 144 Hz (stock's no-pen mode).

         To trade the stylus back for 144 Hz on a running device:
             settings put system peak_refresh_rate 144.0
         The pen stops working immediately, and starts again at 120.

         ★ DO NOT "improve" this into stock's dynamic scheme. That was the
         obvious next step and the device owner rejected it, with the better
         argument: switching costs more than the 24 Hz buys. Every transition is
         a DSI mode change on the panel PLUS a mode change in the touch
         controller, both of which are visible and both of which cost power, and
         they would fire every time the pen enters and leaves range. 144 - 120 is
         1.4 ms of frame time. Paying a mode switch repeatedly to buy that back
         is a bad trade, and pinning the value also deletes a whole class of
         state-machine bugs (pen arriving mid-switch, switch racing the idle
         timer, the chicken-and-egg above). Static is the design, not a stopgap.

         Of the two, config_defaultPeakRefreshRate is the load-bearing one, and
         it is worth knowing why. It is the default of Settings.System
         PEAK_REFRESH_RATE, and updateRefreshRateSettingLocked()
         (DisplayModeDirector.java:1209-1215) turns that into
             Vote.forPhysicalRefreshRates(0, peak)  @ PRIORITY_USER_SETTING_PEAK_REFRESH_RATE
         - a ceiling on the PHYSICAL panel mode, which is what the pen actually
         needs. config_defaultRefreshRate only yields
             Vote.forRenderFrameRates(0, default)   @ PRIORITY_DEFAULT_RENDER_FRAME_RATE
         (:1223-1226), the lowest priority, and it bounds the render rate only -
         it would happily leave the panel in the 144 Hz mode while rendering 120.
         It is set here for coherence, not because it does the work. Confirmed on
         the device: with peak at 120, dumpsys SurfaceFlinger reports
         activeMode={id=2, ... vsyncRate=120.00 Hz}, i.e. the mode really changed.

         Note also that 144 Hz remains in Display > Refresh rate, because that
         list is built at runtime from Display.getSupportedModes()
         (RefreshRateUtils.java:45-49) and no RRO can filter it. Deliberate: the
         alternatives were patching the panel timings in dtbo.img (a partition
         this port never flashes, precisely because ours is byte-identical to
         stock) or carrying the tree's only out-of-device-tree source patch. Apps
         cannot reach 144 regardless - the app-request range only considers votes
         at priority >= APP_REQUEST_REFRESH_RATE_RANGE_PRIORITY_CUTOFF (5) and
         the peak setting sits at 9, so it bounds them. Only a human choosing
         144 in Settings can do it, and the pen starts working again on 120. -->
    <!-- Default to LIGHT, not dark.

         AOSP's own default is already 1 / MODE_NIGHT_NO (config.xml:898). Dark
         mode on this device comes from PixelConfigOverlay2021_GMS, which sets
         this resource to 2 - it is a Pixel behaviour PixelOS ships to every
         device, not something this port asked for.

         It is wrong here on the hardware: this panel is an LCD, not OLED (the
         backlight driver logs [AWNl/E]lcd_brightness_work), so dark mode buys no
         power and costs contrast, and the device owner reported being unable to
         read the UI.

         ⚠️ This line ONLY works because configs/partition_order.xml moves
         /vendor/overlay above /product/overlay. Without that this resource is a
         no-op: `cmd overlay list android` prints in increasing priority and
         android.overlay.malbec came FIRST, below every pixelconfig*_gms. Setting
         it here and stopping would have looked like a fix and changed nothing.
         Verify with:
             adb shell cmd overlay lookup android android:integer/config_defaultNightMode
         which must return 1.

         This is only the DEFAULT; Settings > Display > Dark theme still wins,
         because UiModeManagerService:653 reads Settings.Secure.UI_NIGHT_MODE and
         falls back to this resource only when that is unset. -->
    <integer name="config_defaultNightMode">1</integer>

    <integer name="config_defaultPeakRefreshRate">120</integer>
    <integer name="config_defaultRefreshRate">120</integer>

    <!-- Rounded display corners. Stock gets this from the same vendor RRO as
         config_defaultRefreshRate above; ro.sf.rounded_corner_radius is unset on
         this device, so without an override here SystemUI would not mask the
         corners at all.

         Keeping the unit as px is right: the corner radius is a property of the
         glass, so it must not move when TARGET_SCREEN_DENSITY does.

         The value, though, has been wrong twice, in opposite directions, and
         both times because the dp -> px conversion was anchored to the wrong
         density. Writing the derivation down so it stops happening:

             stock RRO declares    20dp
             stock default density 360   ->  20 * 360/160 = 45 px
             dump-time density     306   ->  20 * 306/160 = 38.25 -> 38 px

         An earlier revision said 45px and cited a dumpsys measurement; that was
         the right number with the wrong evidence. It was then "corrected" to
         38px because work/device_dump/display/dumpsys-display.txt:71 does report
             RoundedCorner{position=TopLeft, radius=38, center=Point(38, 38)}
         and wm.txt records "Override density: 306".

         But 306 was not a stock default and not a menu option misread as a
         value: the owner had set it by hand in Settings > Display size before
         that dump was taken (confirmed by the owner). It says nothing about the
         hardware. Stock's own default is 360: wm.txt:2 says "Physical density:
         360", and dumpsys-display.txt:201 mBaseDisplayInfo density 360 versus
         :202 mOverrideDisplayInfo density 306. So the radius Lenovo dimensioned
         the glass against is 45 px, and 38 was an artefact of one user setting.

         Confirmed directly rather than inferred: aapt2 dump of the stock vendor
         RRO reports
             work/unpacked/parts/vendor/overlay/FrameworksResTarget_Vendor.apk
             -> dimen/rounded_corner_radius  20dp
         and stock's own framework-res.apk leaves it at 0dp, so the RRO is where
         it comes from.

         TARGET_SCREEN_DENSITY is now 360, the same as stock, so 45px and 20dp
         are the same number here. Keep the unit as px anyway: the radius is a
         property of the glass and must not move if the density is ever
         retuned. That is the whole point of expressing it in px.

         ⚠️ Not 156px, which is what work/reference/hirero-malbec-device puts
         here. At 320 real ppi that would be 12.4 mm of corner masking on a
         panel whose vendor RRO says 3.6 mm.

         ★ 60px, not stock's 45px, and this one is an EXPERIMENT rather than a
         derivation - say so plainly so the next person does not read it as
         settled. At 45px the owner photographed a bright crescent of lit panel
         at all four corners (work/s14/corner-white.png, zoomed in
         work/s14/crescent-zoom.png, taken on a solid white screen; there is no
         screen protector on the device, that was checked).

         What the photo does establish: the crescent's INNER edge is visibly
         stair-stepped, i.e. rasterised, so it is a rendered boundary and not an
         optical artefact of the glass; its outer edge is smooth, i.e. physical.
         So SystemUI's mask really is stopping short of where the panel stops
         lighting.

         What it does NOT establish is the sign. Pure geometry says a mask arc
         and a glass arc should never produce a crescent BRIGHTER than the screen
         around it: with mask radius Rm and glass radius Rg, Rg>Rm hides the mask
         entirely (no artefact at all) and Rg<Rm gives a BLACK crescent. A
         brighter-than-screen arc fits neither, so something about the assumed
         geometry is wrong and guessing harder from one oblique photo was not
         going to fix it.

         The value was found by bisection on the device, because the arc model
         I reasoned from did not survive contact: it predicted a black crescent
         for Rm>Rg and no artefact at all for Rm<Rg, and the observed crescent
         was BRIGHTER than the surrounding screen, which is neither. Something
         in that model is wrong and was never found. So this is empirical.

             45px (stock's own 20dp @ 360) -> thin bright crescent, all 4 corners
             60px                          -> crescent GREW  => go down, not up
             36px                          -> "almost gone" (owner, on a white
                                              screen photographed with another
                                              camera)
             35px                          -> this value

         Facts worth not re-deriving:
           - the crescent's inner edge is stair-stepped, i.e. rasterised, so it
             really is SystemUI's mask and not an optical artefact of the bevel;
             its outer edge is smooth, i.e. physical
           - there is no screen protector on this device (asked, confirmed)
           - screencap CANNOT see any of this: AOSP deliberately excludes
             ScreenDecorOverlay from screenshots, so every check needs a photo
             of a solid white screen taken with a second camera. Do not try to
             verify this from a screencap and conclude the corners are square.

         ★ 33px is the device owner's own setting. They walked it down the
         bracket themselves, 35 -> 34 -> 33, living with each one; this is where
         they stopped. Keep it. Going back up above 35 has been established as
         pointless, and below 33 the crescent starts coming back. -->
    <dimen name="rounded_corner_radius">33px</dimen>

    <!-- ★ The status bar's left and right content insets come from HERE, not
         from any SystemUI dimension, and leaving it at AOSP's 0dp default is
         what the device owner saw as "no gap at the left/right edges".

         StatusBarContentInsetsProvider.getCalculatedAreaForRotation()
         (frameworks/base/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/statusbar/
         layout/StatusBarContentInsetsProvider.kt:334-364) is the only place
         those insets are computed, and it computes them as

             roundedCornerPadding = rounded_corner_content_padding
             minLeft  = roundedCornerPadding
             minRight = max(ongoing_appops_dot_min_padding, roundedCornerPadding)

         Note what is NOT in that expression: status_bar_padding_start /
         status_bar_padding_end. Those two are commented out in the same file
         (:100-104, "TODO: Check if this is ever used for devices with no
         rounded corners") and only survive as view padding inside
         PhoneStatusBarView.updatePaddings(). So overriding them is not the knob.

         With rounded_corner_content_padding at 0 the two sides come out
         ASYMMETRIC, because only the right side has a floor:

             left  = 0px
             right = max(ongoing_appops_dot_min_padding = 20dp = 45px, 0) = 45px

         Measured on the device rather than derived: a screencap of the home
         screen puts the clock glyphs ~22px from the left edge (the 4dp
         status_bar_padding_start plus the font's left side bearing) and the
         battery icon ~55px from the right. That 20dp step is visible, and it is
         the whole of the complaint.

         45px restores symmetry, and that is the whole justification: minLeft
         becomes 45px and minRight stays 45px, because 45px is exactly the
         privacy dot floor (ongoing_appops_dot_min_padding = 20dp at density
         360). Any value at or above the dot floor makes the two sides equal;
         45 is the smallest one that does, so it buys symmetry without spending
         more edge margin than necessary.

         ⚠️ Deliberately NOT tied to rounded_corner_radius above, even though
         they were briefly the same number and AOSP's own Pixel overlays do set
         them equal (pixel_7 is 47px radius / 47px padding, pixel_7_pro 50/50;
         PixelOS onyx is 14dp against a 156px radius). The radius is currently
         an unresolved experiment - see its comment - and coupling this to it
         would move the status bar every time the corner mask is probed, which
         is two variables at once. If the radius settles above 45px and the
         corners then look cramped, revisit; until then this stands on the dot
         floor alone.

         Keep the unit px for the same reason as the radius above: it tracks the
         glass, not the density.

         Second consumer, checked before setting this: SystemUI's
         navigation_layout.xml:22-23 and the framework's
         input_method_navigation_layout.xml:22-23 use it as the nav bar's
         start/end margin. On this device the nav bar is gesture mode, so that
         margin only insets the gesture pill's hit region by 20dp per side on a
         1557dp-wide bar - proportionally the same as any Pixel. -->
    <dimen name="rounded_corner_content_padding">45px</dimen>

    <!-- ★ 0.0 here was a black screen waiting to happen.
         DisplayDeviceConfig.java:2057-2071 reads this straight into
         mBacklightMinimum whenever it is not the -2 sentinel, and that is the
         floor below which the brightness slider cannot go. At 0.0 the slider
         bottoms out at backlight 0 on panel0-backlight, i.e. the panel is off.
         The comment above it ("brightnessMinimum 0.0") had copied stock's
         DisplayDeviceInfo.brightnessMinimum, which is in *brightness* space, not
         the resource's space.
         Correct value = BrightnessSynchronizer.brightnessIntToFloat(2)
                       = (2 - 1) / (255 - 1) = 1/254 = 0.003937008,
         derived from stock's config_screenBrightnessSettingMinimum=2 and
         confirmed against the running device: dumpsys power reports
         mScreenBrightnessMinimum=0.003937008.
         brightnessMaximum 1.0 / brightnessDefault 0.39763778 are unchanged. -->
    <item name="config_screenBrightnessSettingMinimumFloat" format="float" type="dimen">0.003937008</item>
    <item name="config_screenBrightnessSettingMaximumFloat" format="float" type="dimen">1.0</item>
    <item name="config_screenBrightnessSettingDefaultFloat" format="float" type="dimen">0.39763778</item>

